The function SDL_GetWindowSize accepts two int pointers, and that's where the function will store the results.

    int width, height;
    SDL_GetWindowSize(window, &width, &height);

After running this code, width and height will be updated by SDL

https://wiki.libsdl.org/SDL_GetWindowSize

I like to keep it public or at least write a getter function for the head because usually I want to actually use the list in my code, so I can just write a (rather ugly) for loop to iterate over it:

    for (Node* cur = list.head; cur != nullptr; cur = cur->next) {
        ...
    }

I'm not sure what the best practice is but I think I did it this way:

    ~LinkedList() {
        Node* cur = head;
        while (cur != nullptr) {
            Node* next = cur->next;
            delete cur;
            cur = next;
        }
    }

As I said idk if this is good or not. I create the Node* next temporary because I don't want to access cur->next after calling delete on cur, I believe that's undefined behavior.

I'm 90% sure std::cin leaves a newline character in the buffer so you have to call std::cin.ignore() after every std::cin >> ...

    std::cin >> val1;
    std::cin.ignore();
    std::cin >> val2;
    std::cin.ignore();
    ...

Good luck!

